.dataframe > thead > tr,
.dataframe > tbody > tr {
  text-align: right;
  white-space: pre-wrap;
}
</style>
<small>shape: (2, 2)</small><table border="1" class="dataframe"><thead><tr><th>literal</th><th>year</th></tr><tr><td>list[str]</td><td>i64</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>[&quot;species&quot;, &quot;island&quot;]</td><td>2007</td></tr><tr><td>[&quot;species&quot;, &quot;island&quot;]</td><td>2007</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
```
:::
:::


A list of expressions there raises `TypeError: … Nested object types`.
Unpack with `*`: `select(*factory(...), "year")`. Keyword arguments beside a
list are fine.

### Same input, same name {#sec-prog-name-trap}

Output names come from the input column
([Naming](../core/expressions.qmd#sec-expr-naming)), so one factory called
twice on one column collides:

::: {#0421f99d .cell execution_count=22}
``` {.python .cell-code}
penguins_df.select(zscore("body_mass_g"), zscore("body_mass_g", by="species"))
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-error}

::: {.ansi-escaped-output}
```{=html}
<pre><span class="ansi-red-fg">DuplicateError</span><span class="ansi-red-fg">:</span> projections contained duplicate output name 'body_mass_g_z'. It's possible that multiple expressions are returning the same default column name. If this is the case, try renaming the columns with `.alias("new_name")` to avoid duplicate column names.
</pre>
```
:::

:::
:::


Rename one at the call site with `.alias()`, or make the suffix a parameter.

### Late binding and shared defaults {#sec-prog-late-binding}

Closures look variables up when called, not when created. An expression
captures *values* as it is built; a UDF lambda in the same loop does not:

::: {#4870b20b .cell execution_count=23}
``` {.python .cell-code}
x_df = pl.DataFrame({"x": [1, 2]})
x_df.select(
    [(pl.col("x") * k).alias(f"native_{k}") for k in (1, 2, 3)]
    + [pl.col("x").map_batches(lambda s: s * k, return_dtype=pl.Int64).alias(f"udf_{k}")
       for k in (1, 2, 3)]
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=23}
```{=html}
<div><style>
